      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 1.1: Distinguish between public health surveillance and public health research.",
      "pageStart": 23,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-learning-objective-11-distinguish-between-public.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "and public health research.\nMichael J. Selgelid\nSession timeline (60 min)\n0–15 min\n(15 min)\n16–20 min\n(5 min)\n21–40 min\n(20 min)\n41–55 min\n(15 min)\n56–60 min\n(5 min)\nIntroduction\nReading\nTeam preparation\nTeam presentations \nand discussion\nSummary and \nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator begins the session by presenting a general overview of the issue, using the slides provided \nas a guide.\nFor facilitators: Include a brief group discussion of the questions raised on the slides. See: http://www.who.\nint/ethics/topics/outbreaks-emergencies/en/.\n2.\t\nThe facilitator distributes sce",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out a 60-minute session on distinguishing public health surveillance from research, with team work on scenarios. The background defines public health and describes surveillance as a core activity that tracks disease patterns and guides control measures. It discusses why the line between research and surveillance is hard to draw, including tensions between informed consent and the goals of surveillance. The section closes with references and further reading.",
