      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 1.2: Identify the scope of activities that could qualify as “research” during emergency response and would normally require research ethics review.",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-learning-objective-12-identify-the-scope-of-acti.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "as “research” during emergency response and would normally require \nresearch ethics review.\nRenaud F. Boulanger and Matthew R. Hunt\nSession timeline (85 min)\n0–30 min\n(30 min)\n31–50 min\n(20 min)\n51–75 min\n(25 min)\n76–90 min\n(15 min)\nIntroduction and small slide presentation\nTeam \npreparation\nTeam presentations \nand discussion\nSummary and\nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator introduces the module and provides a brief background on research ethics committees, \nusing the slides. See: http://www.who.int/ethics/topics/outbreaks-emergencies/en/.\n2.\t\nThe facilitator distributes a copy",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes a session on which activities count as research during emergency response and normally need ethics review, using a team exercise where groups design a project and others judge its type. The background explains that research is usually subject to independent ethics review while public health practice often is not. It outlines categories of research, such as clinical, health services and population-based studies, with examples, followed by references.",
