      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 1.3: Demonstrate understanding of the ethical principles and requirements addressed in current normative instruments relative to research and surveillance in emergencies.",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 44,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-learning-objective-13-demonstrate-understanding.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "principles and requirements addressed in current normative \ninstruments relative to research and surveillance in emergencies.\nGhaiath Hussein\nSession timeline (90 min)\n0–15 min\n(15 min)\n16–35 min\n(20 min)\n36–50 min\n(15 min)\n51–80 min\n(30 min)\n81–90 min\n(10 min)\nIntroduction\nReading\nGroup discussion\nSlide presentation on \nethics guidelines\nDiscussion and \nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator introduces the learning objective (15 min).\nFor facilitators: Ask the participants if they had ever had to use a “research ethics guideline” in their practice \nor if they have heard of such d",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes a 90-minute session on the ethical principles and requirements in current normative instruments for research and surveillance in emergencies. Participants read papers on what makes clinical research ethical, including in developing countries, before a presentation on major research ethics guidelines. The background traces the history of research ethics and the protections it offers participants, while noting debate about whether differences between research and public health practice should decide ethics review. References at the end include material on a disputed drug trial in Nigeria.",
