      "title": "Learning objective 1.4: Identify the shortcomings of current normative instruments for use in emergency situations, and evaluate alternatives.",
      "pageStart": 44,
      "pageEnd": 53,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-learning-objective-14-identify-the-shortcomings.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "instruments for use in emergency situations, and evaluate alternatives.\nGhaiath Hussein\nSession timeline (120 min)\n0–5 min\n(5 min)\n6–10 min\n(5 min)\n11-30 min\n(20 min)\n31–40 min\n(10 min)\n41–55 min\n(15 min)\n56–70 min\n(15 min)\n71–85 min\n(15 min)\n86–110 min\n(25 min)\n111–\n120 min\n(10 min)\nReview of \nlearning \nobjective 1.3\nProbing \nquestions\nReading \nand case \nstudy and \ndiscussion\nReporting \nback\nSlide \npresentation\nReading\nGroup \ndiscussion\nWriting\nSummary \nand \nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator reviews the main guidelines and core principles of research ethics studied in learni",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A training module, part of a course on ethics in emergencies and epidemics, on the shortcomings of existing research ethics guidelines and regulations when applied to emergencies. It gives a 120-minute session plan in which trainees test a guideline against a case study involving issues such as child vulnerability, consent across language barriers, confidentiality and conflicts of interest, then draft their own ethical framework. The background notes that most guidelines were written for clinical research in stable, well-resourced settings, while urgency in emergencies should not excuse ignoring core principles, citing the WHO panel on unregistered Ebola interventions. It then discusses altered perceptions of ethical issues, the patient–provider relationship, study integrity and ethics review, ending with references.",
