      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 2.1: Describe “standard” procedures that should govern an ethics review of research activities, including public health research.",
      "pageStart": 55,
      "pageEnd": 62,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-learning-objective-21-describe-standard-procedur.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "govern an ethics review of research activities, including public health \nresearch.\nKenneth W. Goodman and Sergio Litewka\nSession timeline (90 min)\n0–20 min\n(20 min)\n21–45 min\n(25 min)\n46–70 min\n(25 min)\n71–85 min\n(15 min)\n86–90 min\n(5 min)\nIntroduction\nDebate\nCase study and \ndiscussion\nDiscussion\nSummary and \nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator introduces the module and provides background information on “standard” procedures \nfor ethics review.\n2.\t\nThe facilitator identifies key (conceptual) differences between biomedical research and public health \nresearch.\n3.\t\nThe facilitat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A training module describing the standard procedures that should govern ethics review of research, including public health research. Its 90-minute session plan uses a debate and a case that falls between research and surveillance to ask whether review standards could ever be altered and whether committee review is worth its cost. The background explains why prospective independent review is now broadly accepted, how multidisciplinary committees with lay members work and that review is not meant to obstruct research. It lists eleven areas a review should cover, from social value and informed consent to community participation, risk minimization and international collaboration, and ends with references and further reading.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
