      "id": "library/10665-196326/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 2.2: Identify circumstances in which public health surveillance activities should possibly undergo formal ethics review.",
      "pageStart": 62,
      "pageEnd": 68,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-learning-objective-22-identify-circumstances-in.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "surveillance activities should possibly undergo formal ethics review.\nKenneth W. Goodman\nSession timeline: 75 min\n0–25 min\n(25 min)\n26-30 min\n(5 min)\n31–45 min\n(15 min)\n46–65 min\n(20 min)\n65–75 min\n(10 min)\nIntroduction\nReading\nCase study and \ndiscussion\nDebate\nSummary and \nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator introduces the module.\n2.\t\nThe facilitator splits the group into two and asks participants to explore why surveillance might \nsometimes have to be reviewed by research ethics committees.\nFor facilitators: Emphasize differences between laws and regulations and ethics by exp",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A training module on when public health surveillance should undergo formal ethics review. Its 75-minute session plan uses a hospital testing case and group debate on which kinds of surveillance need review and which do not. The background explores the elusive line between surveillance and research, noting that research usually involves an intervention with quantifiable risk while much surveillance relies on data people may not know are collected. It discusses the ethical basis for oversight, including mutual responsibility, public trust and past misuse of HIV data, and ends by noting calls for risk-based rather than definition-based approaches, followed by references.",
        "state": "draft",
