      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objective 2.3: Describe possible variations to standard procedures of ethics review that are applicable to research in emergencies.",
      "pageStart": 68,
      "pageEnd": 77,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-learning-objective-23-describe-possible-variatio.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "procedures of ethics review that are applicable to research in \nemergencies.\nAthula Sumathipala\nSession timeline (75 min)\n0–5 min\n(5 min)\n6–15 min\n(10 min)\n16–30 min\n(15 min)\n31–40 min\n(10 min)\n41–65 min\n(25 min)\n66–75 min\n(10 min)\nIntroduction\nSmall group \ndiscussion\nGroup discussion\nSlide \npresentation\nCase study and \ndiscussion\nSummary and\nconclusion\nInstruction strategy\n1.\t\nThe facilitator introduces the module and briefly reminds participants of the standard procedures for \nethics review (see learning objective 2.1) (5 min).\n2.\t\nThe facilitator asks participants to reflect on the followin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A training module on possible variations to standard ethics review procedures for research carried out during emergencies. Its 75-minute session plan combines group reflection, slides and two case studies asking whether projects are research or surveillance and whether expedited review or lighter consent procedures are acceptable. The background argues that research in emergencies is needed but must not exploit resource-poor settings, and sets out the debate over whether disaster survivors are inherently vulnerable. Proposed variations include expedited review, proportionality of risks and benefits, special scrutiny, generic anticipatory protocols and proactive review, with references at the end.",
        "state": "draft",
