        "text": "A training module on the harms and benefits that public health practice and surveillance can bring to individuals and communities. Its 105-minute session plan includes writing exercises on when quarantine is justified, use of the Nuffield intervention ladder after an earthquake, a reading on the 1976 swine flu vaccine and a case about a surveillance survey that could fuel stigma. The background explains that, unlike in clinical care, benefits may go to the community while harms fall on individuals, illustrated by quarantine and mandatory vaccination. It stresses ethical practice as a way to build trust, as shown during the 2014 Ebola outbreak, and closes with references.",
