      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A training module on the factors that determine when public health surveillance requires explicit informed consent from individuals or communities. Its 60-minute session plan uses a case of a blood survey for multidrug-resistant tuberculosis, with facilitator notes on whether it is research or surveillance, risks of identification and stigma, what information to disclose and whether patients may refuse. The background defines informed consent, notes its place in the Nuremberg Code, the Declaration of Helsinki and CIOMS guidelines, and sets out the arguments for it. It then considers when consent may be waived or modified, ending with references.",
