        "text": "A training module on the potential harms and benefits to individuals and communities of conducting research during emergencies. Its 60-minute session plan has groups list harms and benefits, optionally view videos on the 1918 influenza pandemic, and discuss conflicting ethical principles and quotations on population health versus individual rights. The background notes that emergency research has sometimes caused disability and death, and raises questions about compromising scientific standards, who bears the risks of unapproved treatments and who gets first access to scarce new treatments. It stresses fair distribution of research benefits and burdens and barriers to valid consent in traditional or low-resource settings, ending with references including reports on drug trials in Nigeria.",
